    "id": "lem:support-cleaning-rate-window",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(\\beta>(d_X+1)/2\\), set \\(a=\\beta/(2\\beta+d_X+1)\\), and choose any fixed \\(\\gamma\\) satisfying \\(1/4<\\gamma<a\\), with \\(\\tau_{S,n}=n^{-\\gamma}\\).  If\n\\[\n r_{A,\\infty}+r_{Z,\\infty}=O_P(\\rho_n),\n \\qquad\n R_{\\Sigma}-r_A^{\\mathrm c}-r_Z^{\\mathrm c}\n =O_P(\\rho_n+\\tau_{S,n}),\n \\qquad r_A+r_Z=O_P(\\rho_n),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n r_{A,\\infty}+r_{Z,\\infty}=o_P(\\tau_{S,n}),\n \\qquad \\tau_{S,n}=o(n^{-1/4}),\n \\qquad R_{\\Sigma}=O_P(\\tau_{S,n}),\n \\qquad \\Delta_n=O_P(\\tau_{S,n}^2)=o_P(n^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThus the support-cleaning requirements are compatible with the declared smoothness regime, although this implication does not assert that the named spline likelihood attains its antecedent rates.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "beta",
      "dX",
      "rho",
      "tauS",
      "n",
      "reta",
      "Rsum",
      "Delta",
      "P"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:quadratic-remainder",
      "lem:support-cleaning-algebra"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The inequality \\(\\beta>(d_X+1)/2\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n a=\\frac{\\beta}{2\\beta+d_X+1}>\\frac14.\n\\]\nHence a fixed \\(\\gamma\\in(1/4,a)\\) exists.  Because \\(\\rho_n=n^{-a}\\) and \\(\\tau_{S,n}=n^{-\\gamma}\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{\\rho_n}{\\tau_{S,n}}=n^{-(a-\\gamma)}\\longrightarrow0,\n \\qquad\n n^{1/4}\\tau_{S,n}=n^{-(\\gamma-1/4)}\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThe first antecedent and (20) give \\((r_{A,\\infty}+r_{Z,\\infty})/\\tau_{S,n}=o_P(1)\\), while the second identity in (20) gives \\(\\tau_{S,n}=o(n^{-1/4})\\).  By definition,\n\\[\n r_A^{\\mathrm c}+r_Z^{\\mathrm c}=r_A+r_Z+2\\tau_{S,n}=O_P(\\tau_{S,n}),\n\\]\nbecause \\(r_A+r_Z=O_P(\\rho_n)=o_P(\\tau_{S,n})\\).  The remaining antecedent then yields \\(R_\\Sigma=O_P(\\tau_{S,n})\\).  Every factor in each product defining \\(\\Delta_n\\) is \\(O_P(\\tau_{S,n})\\), so\n\\[\n \\Delta_n=O_P(\\tau_{S,n}^2).\n\\]\nFinally, \\(n^{1/2}\\tau_{S,n}^2=(n^{1/4}\\tau_{S,n})^2\\to0\\) by (20).  This proves the result and also shows explicitly that it is a rate-propagation statement conditional on, not a proof of, the spline-likelihood antecedents.",
